this is a production of cornell  university  okay hello everyone i'm jacob chose i  work in larry's smart slab in  cornell agritech up in geneva today i'll  be talking about accumulation of  cannabinoids under various stresses and  high cbd hemp  uh presenting some results from what  we've been calling the 2019  hemp stress trial so  hemp is a cannabis sativa plant with  less than 0.3  total thc and the major market for hemp  right now  is cannabidiol or cbd which is already a  billion dollar market  and is expected to grow exponentially  over the next few years  uh cbd is isolated from hemp  inflorescence  and it reaches its highest concentration  in mature unpollinated female brats  um one of the things that a lot of  people are concerned about when growing  hemp for cbd production  is the production of the psychoactive  tetrahydrocannabinol or thc  which is what distinguishes hemp from  marijuana  and there's an arbitrary threshold of  0.3 percent  uh so knowing what results in higher or  lower production of thc is going to be  very important  for growers who want to stay under that  limit and  if you look at sort of what's  popularized in the media or at least was  popular  when these articles were written it  appears that hemp stress is expected to  have  a significant effect on thc so if you  look at the  the hemp and the marijuana blogs uh for  instance here  for tuna hemp uh suggests that growers  need to be aware that plant stresses can  result in thc spikes  rx leaf suggests that uh any plant  stress can tip it over point three  uh cooperative extension says that the  main thing that causes thc spikes are  stressors  and the new york times mentions of the  chemistry of hemp  that can cause them to over produce thc  when stressed  uh so uh while a lot of people  uh think that hemp stress is going to be  very important  it made it all the way to the new york  times  there's very little evidence in the  literature that  links hemp stress and thc production  now part of that's because hemp has only  recently been  uh legalized for production in many  states  uh so what we wanted to do was look  explicitly and see if these sort of  popular sources have any truth to them  that hemp stress is going to  result in higher thc so just looking at  previous work going into this  we didn't expect that hemp stress would  have much  effect on spikes  uh in 2018 we found that there was a  fairly consistent ratio  among genetically similar plants we are  in a little bit of a natural experiment  as well  uh we grew two replicated fields one in  ithaca and one in geneva  and one of our fields was severely  flooded  and you can see here that plant is uh  pretty stressed  but we didn't see any effect on the cbd  dhc ratio  and we didn't see any thc spikes within  genetically similar plants  i know this isn't a perfect experiment  there could have been other factors at  play  and there may have even been some  selection bias as the plants that were  really stress  here uh just died so what we wanted to  do  was run a randomized complete block  design experiment to  see if stress affects the thc levels and  if stress affects this cbd to thc  ratio so to do this we had three  cultivars  in four reps with six total treatments  which i'll discuss in a minute  and we took shoot tip samples every week  for four weeks  and then um using hplc looked  at the cannabinoids that were produced  so we had six treatments we had our  control plants which were grown under  best practices under black plastic we  had powdery mildew which is a fungal  pathogen which we thought could induce a  response  rather surprisingly we're not 100 sure  on the evolutionary purpose of  cannabinoids it's been suggested that  they might be a factor in uh pest  response or pathogen response or maybe  even  uv response uh so we included powdery  mildew because we thought  it might induce more cannabinoids there  uh mechanical damage is something that  people are concerned about  it may hail there may be some wind  damage or  potential herbivory so we included that  as a potential stress  and to generate that stress we damaged  the plants for the grass trimmer  we had a flooding treatment with  saturated the soil regularly  and this was largely to repeat that 2018  finding in a controlled  experiment we also included two chemical  treatments  ethaphon which was found to increase  total cannabinoids  in a greenhouse study of marijuana and a  herbicide  at a low concentration to any light  herbicide drift  so that's what you see here these plants  have been sprayed with 0.5 percent  roundup  and they're pretty stressed  so this is just the control this is best  practices and this matches  a lot of the other data that we've been  seeing the cbd  increases over time and it's somewhat  cultivar dependent  uh so it started at week zero before  application of these  stresses all the cultivars are around  around three percent total cbd and then  at the end of the experiment  the cam or the cbd has either leveled  out in that one case  or is around 10 percent which is usually  where  growers aim to take the crop  so that's cbd and if we look at thc  the graph is actually very similar so if  you look at this total potential cbd and  this little inset  and the total potential cbd in this  larger  graph they look very similar very  similar ratio of cbd to thc over  time uh what's also very noteworthy  looking at this  is that these two cultivars the blue and  the red  they're significantly over that point  three percent thc  uh even under best practices as  unstressed as we could get them  so if the there's a very similar ratio  of cbd to thc  we'd expect that even without any  additional stresses or anything else  uh at around seven to eight percent cbd  we'd be getting non-compliant levels of  thc  and under some circumstances the crop  might have to be completely discarded  uh so that's in the control we wanted to  see if there was any effect  of any of these stresses on either the  ratio or the total amount of  cannabinoids  um and largely there was very  small effect of stress uh so if you look  at this total potential cbd  you can see that most of these  treatments cluster with the control  except for herbicide which is generally  a little lower and given how  stressed those plants are that's not all  together surprising the plants were  very significantly um  stressed they were they were fairly  close to death we had trouble finding  green tissue to  sample if you look at the total  potential thc  it's a very similar story it looks like  the ratios are about the same throughout  you see lower herbicide um and that's  we think largely because of uh just sort  of  general stress the plants are nearly  dead we also  did not see any huge spikes in thc  which is sometimes what has been  reported um  but when we look just at the harvest  time here and look at it statistically  we can see this total thc the only  significant  difference um compared to the control is  that the herbicide  has lower total thc so  it appears that stress does not cause  thc to spike  what's more if we look at this ratio of  total cbd over total thc  this ratio remains the same in all  comparisons  at harvest to explain why that happens  i'm going to talk a little bit about  the production of cannabinoids so  first off the first cannabinoid that  gets synthesized is  cvga and the amount of cbga that gets  synthesized is going to be a function  of a few things like trichome density  um production of this olaptonic acid  precursor  um activity of this got enzyme a whole  bunch of things like that  and a lot of these things apparently  have a strong genetic face is what we've  seen these stresses didn't affect them  too much  but can be slowed down by uh  nearly killing the plant with herbicide  so what happens in  hemp with cbd asymptotes is the cbga  gets converted to cdda which  can then be converted to cbd and in  marijuana  uh you have thca synthase which converts  to cbga to thca  which then gets converted to thc  so as i pointed out a few times these  hemp plants which  should only have this cbda synthase  produce a fairly consistent ratio of  cbda to thca  and as it turns out if you isolate that  cbda synthase or express it in a yeast  system  and incubate it with the cbga you get a  small amount of production  of this thc as well and what's uh  very encouraging is that the ratio that  you see in vitro is very similar to the  ratio that we see  in vivo so these hemp plants that were  growing that thca  is the result of promiscuous activity of  the cbda  synthase enzyme and none of these  stresses  apparently affect that promiscuous  activity  of the enzyme so  thc spikes are a real thing it's just a  lot of people have been attributing it  to stress  um while we could not see any uh spikes  due to stress  and um to summarize why we think that is  just in a few words is because those  plants  have active thca synthase enzyme in them  uh so cannabinoid type can be accurately  modeled by a single locus called the b  locus it comes in two major flavors the  bt  which has the active thca synthase and  the bd  which has active cbda synthase and since  hemp is diploid  you can get three chemotypes as they're  called bt dt  which produces mainly thc btbd  which produces significant amounts of  both and then chemotype 3  bdbd which is what you would think  most cbd hemp should be but  a lot of the commercially available  cultivars are not solely chemotype 3  a lot of them have significant numbers  of chemotype 2  and some even have some chemotype 1. so  here are  four different cultivars that we grew  this first one here that's all blue is  all chemotypes three  the next one has a little bit of  chemotype two  and this one in the bottom corner was in  fact two-thirds chemotype  two so unsurprisingly it produced a lot  of thc  now if you were just randomly sampling  you might  randomly pick a plant that's blue  chemotype 3  and then you know a week later you pick  one that's  orange chemotype 2 and blame it on the  stress  when in fact it was simply a genetic  phenomenon  it was just a different chemotype due to  a different gene at that b locus  so in summary stress has the potential  to alter cannabinoid content for  instance if you nearly kill the plant  with herbicide  cannabinoids will be lower but the  effect was generally fairly minor  very importantly the stress does not  affect the cbd to thc ratio at farthest  um and we think that the spikes that  people have been reporting  are likely due to sampling different  chemotypes as we're seeing that  chemotypes  uh segregation is very common in many  cultivars  another important result is that high  cbd plants are almost certain to test  above 0.3  thc when grown to a high cbd percentage  so what we've been advising growers to  do is to ensure that they're growing  seeds from a  reputable source that ideally don't have  any of that active thca synthase enzyme  and also to not get too greedy to test  and harvest before the plants get too  high  of level of cbd uh so that can remain  below  that threshold of thc  uh so for acknowledgements i'd like to  thank other members of the larry smart  lab particularly george stack and craig  carlson  i'd like to thank chris smartslav and  ali khala  for their work with powdery mildew and  the rose lab for working with  um the hplc to determine these  cannabinoids  uh this work was largely funded by new  york state department of ag and markets  and pixas international  and uh with that if i have any time left  i'd be happy to take some questions  great uh thank you for that um  really thorough talk jacob if anybody  has any questions they can  either unmute or put them in the chat  and make sure to fill out that feedback  form i put the link  in the chat as well  hey jacob this is this is travis  what is the amount of heat that's needed  to convert the  the precursor to cbd into cbd  right so cbda and thca  get converted into the active forms of  cbd and thc  and there's no if you look at the  decarboxylation profiles  they're temperature dependent so we've  had samples that have been left at room  temperature for a year  and they end up being mostly  decarboxylated  but one of the reasons why marijuana is  smoked is because high temperatures it  gets  decarboxylated very quickly so if  there's a  time temperature dependence and  they are different for different  cannabinoids this decarboxylation  and that's an area of active research  okay thanks  i think there's one more question in the  chat before we  move on uh faith asks have you  considered including mechanical stress  for another experiment so um  we were hoping that our uh mechanical  damaging the plant would sort of  encompass um any sort of major  mechanical stress  um it might be something different  um given that we saw very  clear no results i don't think we have  immediate plans  to um repeat work like this  uh we have a nice theory that it's just  that one enzyme  and we don't foresee it being affected  by  mechanical stress and we didn't see an  effect of our physical damage  so it would be another stress that could  potentially be included  but we uh don't really have any  immediate plans to test that  awesome uh thank you again jacob we can  all give him uh  the clap emojis um and i think ana's  gonna introduce our next speaker  this has been a production of cornell  university  on the web at cornell.edu

