 any of these stresses on either the  ratio or the total amount of  cannabinoids  um and largely there was very  small effect of stress uh so if you look  at this total potential cbd  you can see that most of these  treatments cluster with the control  except for herbicide which is generally  a little lower and given how  stressed those plants are that's not all  together surprising the plants were  very significantly um  stressed they were they were fairly  close to death we had trouble finding  green tissue to  sample if you look at the total  potential thc  it's a very similar story it looks like  the ratios are about the same throughout  you see lower herbicide um and that's  we think largely because of uh just sort  of  general stress the plants are nearly  dead we also  did not see any huge spikes in thc  which is sometimes what has been  reported um  but when we look just at the harvest  time here and look at it statistically  we can see this total thc the only  significant  difference um compared to the control is  that the herbicide  has lower total thc so  it appears that stress does not cause  thc to spike  what's more if we look at this ratio of  total cbd over total thc  this ratio remains the same in all  comparisons  at harvest to explain why that happens  i'm going to talk a little bit about  the production of cannabinoids so  first off the first cannabinoid that  gets synthesized is  cvga and the amount of cbga that gets  synthesized is going to be a function  of a few things like trichome density  um production of this olaptonic acid  precursor  um activity of this got enzyme a whole  bunch of things like that  and a lot of these things apparently  have a strong genetic face is what we've  seen these stresses didn't affect them  too much  but can be slowed down by uh  nearly killing the plant with herbicide  so what happens in  hemp with cbd asymptotes is the cbga  gets converted to cdda which  can then be converted to cbd and in  marijuana  uh you have thca synthase which converts  to cbga to thca  which then gets converted to thc  so as i pointed out a few times these  hemp plants which  should only have this cbda synthase  produce a fairly consistent ratio of  cbda to thca  and as it turns out if you isolate that  cbda synthase or express it in a yeast  system  and incubate it with the cbga you get a  small amount of production  of this thc as well and what's uh  very encouraging is that the ratio that  you see in vitro is very similar to the  ratio that we see  in vivo so these hemp plants that were  growing that thca  is the result of promiscuous activity of  the cbda  synthase enzyme and none of these  stresses  apparently affect that promiscuous  activity  of the enzyme so  thc spikes are a real thing it's just a  lot of people have been attributing it  to stress  um while we could not see any uh spikes  due to stress  and um to summarize why we think that is  just in a few words is because those  plants  have active thca synthase enzyme in them  uh so cannabinoid type can be accurately  modeled by a single locus called the b  locus it comes in two major flavors the  bt  which has the active thca synthase and  the bd  which has active cbda synthase and since  hemp is diploid  you can get three chemotypes as they're  called bt dt  which produces mainly thc btbd  which produces significant amounts of  both and then chemotype 3  bdbd which is what you would think  most cbd hemp should be but  a lot of the commercially available  cultivars are not solely chemotype 3  a lot of them have significant numbers  of chemotype 2  and some even have some chemotype 1. so  here are  four different cultivars that we grew  this first one here that's all blue is  all chemotypes three  the next one has a little bit of  chemotype two  and this one in the bottom corner was in  fact two-thirds chemotype  two so unsurprisingly it produced a lot  of thc  now if you were just randomly sampling  you might  randomly pick a plant that's blue  chemotype 3  and then you know a week later you pick  one that's  orange chemotype 2 and blame it on the  stress  when in fact it was simply a genetic  phenomenon  it was just a different chemotype due to  a different gene at that b locus  so in summary stress has the potential  to alter cannabinoid content for  instance if you nearly kill the plant  with herbicide  cannabinoids will be lower but the  effect was generally fairly minor  very importantly the stress does not  affect the cbd to thc ratio at farthest  um and we think that the spikes that  people have been reporting  are likely due to sampling different  chemotypes as we're seeing that  chemotypes  uh segregation is very common in many  cultivars  another important result is that high  cbd plants are almost certain to test  above 0.3  thc when grown to a high cbd percentage  so what we've been advising growers to  do is to ensure that they're growing  seeds from a  reputable source that ideally don't have  any of that active thca synthase enzyme  and also to not get too greedy to test  and harvest before the plants get too  high  of level of cbd uh so that can remain  below  that threshold of thc  uh so for acknowledgements i'd like to  thank other members of the larry smart  lab particularly george stack and craig  carlson  i'd like to thank chris smartslav and  ali khala  for their work with powdery mildew and  the rose lab for working with  um the hplc to determine these  cannabinoids  uh this work was largely funded by new  york state department of ag and markets  and pixas international  and uh with that if i have any time left  i'd be happy to take some questions  great uh thank you for that um  really thorough talk jacob if anybody  has any questions they can  either unmute or put them in the chat  and make sure to fill out that feedback  form i put the link  in the chat as well  hey jacob this is this is travis  what is the amount of heat that's needed  to convert the  the precursor to cbd into cbd  right so cbda and thca  get converted into the active forms of  cbd and thc  and there's no if you look at the  decarboxylation profiles  they're temperature dependent so we've  had samples that have been left at room  temperature for a year  and they end up being mostly  decarboxylated  but one of the reasons why marijuana is  smoked is because high temperatures it  gets  decarboxylated very quickly so if  there's a  time temperature dependence and  they are different for different  cannabinoids this decarboxylation  and that's an area of active research  okay thanks  i think there's one more question in the  chat before we  move on uh faith asks have you  considered including mechanical stress  for another experiment so um  we were hoping that our uh mechanical  damaging the plant would sort of  encompass um any sort of major  mechanical stress  um it might be something different  um given that we saw very  clear no results i don't think we have  immediate plans  to um repeat work like this  uh we have a nice theory that it's just  that one enzyme  and we don't foresee it being affected  by  mechanical stress and we didn't see an  effect of our physical damage  so it would be another stress that could  potentially be included  but we uh don't really have any  immediate plans to test that  awesome uh thank you again jacob we can  all give him uh  the clap emojis um and i think ana's  gonna introduce our next speaker  this has been a production of cornell  university  on the web at cornell.edu


